-- allium: 3
-- todo.allium
--
-- Scope: Personal TODO overlay — a lightweight checklist, separate from the
--        kanban board. Extracted from core.allium; extends it with linked
--        task/epic references.
-- Excludes: Task and Epic lifecycle (see tasks.allium, epics.allium); the
--           shared domain model (see core.allium). Which rows a board is sent
--           and how they get there (see sync.allium) — this file says whose a
--           todo is, not how that fact is transported.

use "./core.allium" as core

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A Todo is a lightweight personal checklist item, separate from the kanban
-- board. sort_order gives an explicit user-controllable ordering; done marks
-- completion. The view sorts done items below open ones.
entity Todo {
    title: String

    -- The person this checklist belongs to. Stamped at creation from the local
    -- install's identity and never rewritten afterwards.
    --
    -- WHY A TODO HAS AN OWNER AND A TASK DOES NOT ALWAYS. core/Task.owner is
    -- set exactly when the task has no epic, because an epic task belongs to
    -- the epic and everyone following that epic should see it. A todo has no
    -- epic to belong to and nothing to share it through — the overlay is called
    -- "personal" in the line above and that is not decoration. Without this
    -- field the only query a shared store can answer for todos is "all of
    -- them", which puts a colleague's shopping list on your checklist.
    --
    -- Null only on a todo created before this install ever connected: the
    -- identity is minted by the store on first connection and does not exist
    -- before then (core/Host.owner says the same thing about the same moment).
    --
    -- A NULL OWNER IS A TODO NO SHARED BOARD WILL EVER SHOW, and today nothing
    -- fills one in. sync.allium's subscription asks for the todos of one
    -- identity and a null owner is not that identity; the schema migration that
    -- introduced this column stamped the todos that existed when it ran, but
    -- anything created after it on a still-unconnected install stays null
    -- forever. That is a real gap, not a transitional one — see the open
    -- question at the foot of this file.
    owner: core/UserIdentity?
    done: Boolean = false
    sort_order: Integer = 0

    -- Link to exactly one board item, or neither. At most one of linked_task /
    -- linked_epic is set; both null means the todo is unlinked. The domain layer
    -- exposes the pair as a single discriminated reference (TodoLink, which is
    -- either a Task link or an Epic link) — the two fields here mirror how the
    -- link is stored, and the discriminated form is what rules and the UI see.
    linked_task: core/Task?
    linked_epic: core/Epic?

    -- null means this is a root todo; set means it is a child nested under that
    -- root. Nesting is limited to a single level (no grandchildren) — a todo
    -- whose parent is set may not itself be the parent of any other todo.
    -- Deleting a parent promotes its children to roots rather than cascading the
    -- delete: each child's parent is cleared.
    parent: Todo?

    created_at: Timestamp

    -- Relationships
    children: Todo with parent = this

    -- Derived
    is_root: parent = null

    invariant AtMostOneLink {
        not (linked_task != null and linked_epic != null)
    }

    invariant NestingDepthOne {
        parent != null implies children.count = 0
    }

    @invariant TodoOwnerIsWrittenOnce
        -- A todo's owner goes null -> some identity at most once, and never
        -- moves again.
        --
        -- TWO WRITERS, BOTH GUARDED. CreateTodo stamps a new row, and the
        -- schema migration that introduced the column stamped the rows that
        -- already existed — a one-shot `WHERE owner IS NULL` against this
        -- install's stored identity. The migration is named here because it is
        -- the one that actually backfills anything today, and a spec that
        -- listed only CreateTodo would be describing a system with no history.
        --
        -- The same shape as core/Host.owner's LocalHostOwnerIsWrittenOnce, and
        -- not stated as an expression for the same reason: the claim is about
        -- the field's HISTORY, and an invariant is evaluated against a single
        -- state. What is checkable is that no rule writes it twice — nothing on
        -- the update path reaches this field at all.
        --
        -- The point of saying it out loud is that a second write could only be
        -- one machine claiming another person's checklist, which is exactly
        -- what this field was added to prevent.

    @guidance
        -- AtMostOneLink is upheld by the domain type (the discriminated
        -- TodoLink), not by a database constraint: SQLite's ALTER TABLE ADD
        -- COLUMN cannot add a table-level CHECK, so both columns were added
        -- unconstrained. The read path is defensive rather than strict — a row
        -- carrying both links resolves to the task link and the epic link is
        -- silently dropped.
        --
        -- NestingDepthOne is enforced by the two nesting actions (NestTodo
        -- refuses to nest under a child, and refuses to re-nest a child), not by
        -- a constraint. Deleting a parent clears its children's parent rather
        -- than deleting them.
        --
        -- CreateTodo: a new Todo is appended at the end of the list. Its
        --   sort_order is strictly greater than every existing Todo's
        --   sort_order (max(sort_order) + 1), and done is false. Its owner is
        --   the local install's identity, or null where the install has never
        --   connected.
        --
        -- A NOTE ON THE OVERLAY WHILE A STORE IS CONFIGURED. Every mutation
        --   above writes the local store, and sync.allium has moved the board's
        --   READS to the subscription — so on a configured board a toggle or a
        --   reorder changes nothing on screen, and ReorderTodo computes sibling
        --   order from a row set that is not the one being drawn. This is the
        --   same split sync.allium's open question about writes describes, and
        --   it is recorded here too because the overlay is where somebody would
        --   first notice it.
        --   The optional link is set at creation time (auto-linked
        --   from the board selection when using quick-add).
        --
        -- ToggleTodoDone: toggling flips done (false -> true, true -> false).
        --   No other field changes.
        --
        -- Display order: the overlay renders todos hierarchically, not by raw
        --   sort_order. Roots come first (open roots before done roots), and
        --   each root is immediately followed by its children (open children
        --   before done children). Orphans — children whose parent is missing
        --   from the rendered set — sort to the end. Children render indented
        --   two spaces under their parent.
        --
        -- ReorderTodo: reordering swaps a Todo's sort_order with that of its
        --   nearest sibling in the desired direction, where "sibling" is scoped
        --   to the same group. A root swaps with the nearest root in that
        --   direction, skipping over any children that lie between them; a child
        --   swaps only with another child under the same parent. Exactly two
        --   rows change; all other Todos are untouched. A reorder past either
        --   end of the group is a no-op.
        --
        -- ClearDoneTodos: clearing removes every Todo whose done is true.
        --   Open Todos (done = false) are untouched.
        --
        -- LinkTodo: sets the link on an existing todo. Replaces any previous
        --   link. The user picks the target by browsing the board in
        --   InputMode::LinkTodoToTask; only Task and Epic items are linkable.
        --
        -- UnlinkTodo: clears the link on an existing todo (clears both linked
        --   fields). The todo remains; only the association is removed.
        --
        -- NestTodo: bound to Tab on the selected todo. Nests the selected todo
        --   under the todo immediately above it in display order, setting
        --   parent to that todo. Preconditions: the todo above must be
        --   a root (parent = null), since nesting is limited to one level;
        --   and the selected todo must itself be a root (a child cannot be
        --   re-nested under a different parent via Tab). If there is no todo
        --   above, or the todo above is already a child, this is a no-op.
        --
        -- UnnestTodo: bound to Shift+Tab on the selected todo. Promotes a child
        --   todo back to a root by clearing its parent.
        --   A no-op when the selected todo is already a root.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "Who fills a null Todo.owner once an install first connects? Nothing does today. The schema migration stamped the rows that existed when it ran, and every todo created after it on a still-unconnected install stays unowned — which, once that install connects, means a checklist that is permanently invisible to its own owner. The two candidates are a client-side backfill at the moment the identity is adopted, beside host.allium's AdoptUserIdentity, and the one-time seed. Neither is implemented, and the choice decides whether those todos are recoverable at all."
